        heading: 'Leaving Oklahoma',
        body:
          'Drought, dust, and the bank have finished tenant farming in eastern Oklahoma. The Joads have worked the same land for generations, but a tractor can do the job for less, and the family is pushed off by men who insist that nobody in particular is responsible, that the bank is a monster nobody owns. Tom Joad, newly paroled after serving time for killing a man in a fight, walks home along a dusty road to find the house empty, one corner shoved off its foundation, and the family already gone.\n\nHe finds them at his uncle’s place, loading everything they own onto a cut-down Hudson: mattresses, a bucket, Ma’s small box of letters and keepsakes, the pig they have just salted down. Jim Casy, the preacher who used to hold revival meetings in that country and has since given it up, asks to come along, and Ma settles the question by making room for him. Grampa swears he will squash California grapes against his face and let the juice run off his chin, then refuses to leave at the last moment and has to be dosed with soothing syrup and carried aboard.\n\nWhat sends them west is a handbill promising good wages for fruit pickers. Nobody in the family asks how many identical handbills have been printed, or why a grower would advertise so widely for so few jobs. They turn onto Route 66 with the truck sagging under the whole family and everything it owns, carrying a hope that is enormous and almost entirely unearned.',
