        body:
          'The road is lined with families exactly like the Joads, and it takes a price at every stage. Grampa suffers a stroke and dies the first night out, buried in a field with a note in a fruit jar so that the body will not be taken for a murder victim. Granma dies crossing the desert, and Ma lies beside her in the dark for hours saying nothing, because stopping at the inspection station would have cost the family the crossing. Noah wanders off along the green Colorado River and does not come back, and Connie, who has been describing a future of correspondence courses and a job in town, simply disappears.\n\nCalifornia, when they finally see it from the pass, is beautiful and closed. There are many more men than jobs, and the growers know it; the handbills were printed by the thousand precisely so that hungry men would bid one another down. The Joads move between roadside camps and squatter settlements called Hoovervilles, where a deputy can burn a camp on a whim and the word "Okie," which once meant only that a man came from Oklahoma, has become something you say to make a person smaller.\n\nThe exception is the government camp, with its sanitary units, its elected committees, and its Saturday night dances policed by the campers themselves. For a few weeks the Joads are treated as citizens rather than vermin, and Ma stands straighter for it. But there is no steady work within reach, and hunger eventually pushes them back onto the road, which is Steinbeck’s argument in miniature: dignity is not a private virtue but a set of conditions, and conditions can be arranged.',
