          'At the Hooper ranch the Joads pick peaches behind a police escort, not understanding at first that they are strikebreakers, or that the five cents a box they are paid will drop to two and a half as soon as the strike is broken. Tom slips out under the wire at night and finds Jim Casy leading the strikers from a culvert. Casy has finished his long argument with himself and now speaks for the men rather than for God, and when the vigilantes come he says only that they do not know what they are doing before a pick handle crushes his skull. Tom takes the handle, kills the man who swung it, and comes back to the family with his face broken open and a warrant closing in.\n\nThe family hides him first in a culvert and then in the cotton fields, living in a boxcar and picking until Ruthie, boasting to another child during a quarrel, gives away the fact that her brother killed a man. Tom leaves. His parting words to Ma, spoken in the dark of a cave of vines, turn Casy’s idea into a promise: he will be wherever hungry people are fighting to eat, wherever a cop is beating a man, in the way children laugh when they finally have supper. It is the moment the novel completes its argument, transferring the preacher’s soul into the fugitive’s.\n\nThe rains come, the cotton ends, and the boxcar camp floods. Rose of Sharon, weakened by hunger and abandonment, delivers a stillborn child, and Uncle John sets the small body afloat in the stream to carry the family’s accusation downstream. The survivors wade to high ground and find a barn holding a starving man and his son. Rose of Sharon looks at her mother, understands what is being asked without a word passing between them, and sends the others outside; she draws the dying stranger’s head to her breast and smiles a smile the narrator calls mysterious. On that image, with nothing resolved and no rescue promised, the book stops.',
