        heading: 'Dignity, family, and endurance',
        body:
          'Almost everything in the migrants’ world is arranged to make them smaller: the word "Okie" spat as an insult, the camps burned by deputies, wages set low enough that a man must beg for the privilege of working. Against this the Joads set an obstinate insistence on the ordinary forms of decency. Ma refuses to be spoken to as though she were dirt and refuses to let the family break apart even when Pa has given up, and Grampa must be buried properly even though there is no money and no preacher willing to call himself one.\n\nSteinbeck is honest about how thin the margin is. Uncle John drinks, Noah walks away, Connie deserts his pregnant wife, and Pa is reduced to near silence once he can no longer provide. Dignity in this novel is not a personal quality that some people happen to possess; it has to be defended daily, and it can be lost through simple exhaustion. That is precisely why the government camp matters so much, since clean water and an elected committee restore in a week what months on the road had worn away.\n\nWhat survives at the end is not the family as an institution but the impulse that held it together. The Joads as a unit are broken: dead, scattered, hunted, reduced to a handful of people in a barn. Yet Ma feeds strangers, Tom speaks for men he has never met, and Rose of Sharon nurses a dying stranger, so that the loyalty once reserved for kin has been redistributed. Steinbeck suggests that this is the only form of endurance available to people who have lost everything else.',
