        body:
          'Steinbeck writes in two voices and keeps them both in the same book. One is documentary and flat, recording exactly what a tenant farmer says to a tractor driver or what a used car costs, with dialogue rendered in Oklahoma speech so consistently that the page acquires a sound. The other is incantatory, built on repetition and parallel clauses, closer to the King James Bible than to a newspaper. The famous passage about the vintage belongs to the second voice, and it works only because the first voice has already earned the reader’s trust.\n\nThe scriptural frame runs deeper than the prose rhythm. The migration west repeats the Exodus, complete with a promised land that turns out to be occupied; Casy carries the initials and the fate of a martyr; the closing scene borrows the vocabulary of communion. Steinbeck uses these parallels to give a wage dispute the weight of scripture, which is a considerable rhetorical gamble.\n\nWhat keeps the gamble from collapsing is his refusal to let symbolism replace fact. The turtle is a real turtle before it is anything else, the peaches are picked at five cents a box, and the flood is made of actual water rising under an actual boxcar. The grandeur is always fastened to something a reader can see, which is why the book still reads as testimony rather than sermon.',
