    aboutAuthor:
      'John Steinbeck (1902–1968) grew up in Salinas, California, in the farming valley that supplied the settings for much of his best work, and he spent stretches of his early adulthood doing manual labor alongside the kinds of people he would later write about. Before *The Grapes of Wrath* he had already published *Of Mice and Men* and *In Dubious Battle*, both concerned with agricultural workers, so when the migrant crisis of the late 1930s arrived he was unusually well prepared to write about it.\n\nHe reported the book before he wrote it. Steinbeck traveled through the migrant camps of California, published a series of newspaper articles on conditions there, and drew on the knowledge of a federal camp manager who showed him how such a camp worked from the inside. That research is visible on every page, in the prices, the wages, and the mechanics of a used car sale, and it is the reason the novel could not simply be dismissed as invention, although a great many people tried.\n\nPublication in 1939 made him famous and hated in roughly equal measure. Growers’ associations denounced the book, libraries and school boards banned it, and copies were burned in places it described, while it simultaneously sold in enormous numbers and won the Pulitzer Prize. Steinbeck received the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1962, with this novel prominent among the works cited, and throughout his career he remained a writer drawn to the people that official histories tend to leave out.',
