        body:
          'A stranger appears at the edge of the crowd on the day of Hester’s public shaming, a small, stooped man with one shoulder higher than the other, and Hester recognizes her husband. He extracts a promise from her not to reveal who he is, takes the name Roger Chillingworth, settles in Boston as a physician, and tells her he will find the child’s father by other means. He is confident, and he turns out to be right.\n\nDimmesdale, meanwhile, is wasting away. His congregation reads his pallor and his habit of pressing a hand to his heart as evidence of holiness, and his sermons grow more powerful the more his conscience tortures him, which is itself a further torture. Chillingworth attaches himself to the sick minister first as physician and then as housemate, working on him with sympathy and small questions, until one afternoon he opens the sleeping man’s shirt, sees something on his chest, and is seized by a wild, horrible joy.\n\nWhat follows is less a revenge than a slow cultivation. Chillingworth does not expose Dimmesdale, because exposure would end the pleasure; he keeps the wound open instead. Hester, watching from outside, sees what her promise has cost, and comes to believe that she was wrong to make it, and that this deliberate poisoning of another man’s soul is a far worse offense than the one for which she wears the letter.',
