        body:
          'Seven years pass. Hester’s charity and competence gradually alter what her letter is understood to mean, and some in the town begin saying the "A" stands for "Able." At midnight Dimmesdale climbs the scaffold alone in a private rehearsal of confession, and Hester and Pearl join him there in the dark; a meteor burns a shape across the sky that the minister reads as a letter meant for him, though the town later interprets it as an "A" for the angel of a governor who has just died.\n\nIn the forest Hester finally tells Dimmesdale the truth about Chillingworth. For an hour they plan an escape to Europe together, and she takes off the letter and lets down her hair, and sunlight comes into the glade. Pearl refuses to cross the brook to them until the letter is back on her mother’s breast, which is the novel’s verdict on the plan before the plot delivers its own: Chillingworth has already booked passage on the same ship.\n\nOn Election Day, having preached the greatest sermon of his life, Dimmesdale turns aside from the procession, calls Hester and Pearl up to the scaffold, and confesses before the whole town. He tears open his shirt, and the onlookers see, or believe they see, something written there; Hawthorne records their competing accounts and refuses to arbitrate. Dimmesdale dies in Hester’s arms, Chillingworth withers within the year having lost the only thing that organized his life, and Pearl inherits his property and appears to marry abroad. Hester returns to Boston long afterward of her own accord, resumes the letter, and is buried at last beside Dimmesdale beneath a single slab bearing a heraldic motto: "ON A FIELD, SABLE, THE LETTER A, GULES."',
