          'Pearl acts on an inner law nobody else can follow, delighting in the letter on her mother’s breast, throwing wildflowers at it, asking the questions the adults have silently agreed not to ask. What she wants, insistently and without having a word for it, is for the truth to be spoken aloud. When Dimmesdale offers affection in private she demands to know whether he will stand with them at noonday, and she will accept nothing less. Her demands are unreasonable in the ordinary sense and exactly right in the moral one.',
        arc:
          'For most of the novel Pearl is more emblem than child, described as the scarlet letter given life, and she behaves with an uncanniness that unsettles everyone including her mother. She resists every attempt to make her ordinary, refusing to cross the brook while the letter is off, refusing to be kissed by a father who will not claim her. The change comes on the scaffold: when Dimmesdale acknowledges her publicly and she kisses him, the narrator says her tears fall on her father’s cheek as a pledge that she will grow up to be a woman in the world rather than forever at war with it. She ends the novel offstage, wealthy and apparently married abroad, which is the most ordinary fate anyone in the book receives.',
