    aboutAuthor:
      'Nathaniel Hawthorne (1804–1864) was born in Salem, Massachusetts, into a family whose New England roots reached back to the first generation of Puritan settlers. One ancestor had persecuted Quakers and another sat as a judge at the Salem witch trials, and Hawthorne wrote about that inheritance with a mixture of fascination and shame; he is generally believed to have added the "w" to the family name partly in order to set himself slightly apart from it. His father, a ship’s captain, died abroad when Hawthorne was a small boy, and he grew up in a household of women in a town saturated with its own history.\n\nHe spent years writing stories that almost nobody read, published early work anonymously, and supported himself with government appointments, including a post at the Salem Custom House that he lost when the political winds changed. That dismissal proved productive: he wrote *The Scarlet Letter* quickly afterward and framed it with the autobiographical preface set in the Custom House itself. Published in 1850, the novel made his reputation almost immediately and remains the work by which he is known.\n\nHawthorne stood near the center of the American literary flowering of the 1850s. He lived for a time at Brook Farm, the utopian community he would later satirize in fiction; he knew Emerson and Thoreau in Concord; and he formed an intense friendship with Herman Melville, who dedicated *Moby-Dick* to him. His other major books, among them *The House of the Seven Gables*, share the preoccupations of his masterpiece: inherited guilt, the weight of the past, and the distance between what a community sees and what is actually there.',
