        arc:
          'Jim enters as the target of a boy’s prank and becomes the moral adult of the novel. The turn comes after the fog, when Huck tries to convince him he dreamed the separation and Jim, discovering the trick, says that people who put dirt on a friend’s head are trash, and Huck humbles himself to apologize for the first time in his life. Later Jim tells the story of striking his small daughter for disobeying an order she could not hear, not knowing she had gone deaf, which is the deepest grief anyone in the book expresses. Twain then subjects him to the humiliations of the Phelps farm, a choice many readers find indefensible, and gives him as a last word the decision to surrender his freedom rather than leave a wounded boy.',
