    aboutAuthor:
      'Charlotte Brontë (1816–1855) grew up in the parsonage at Haworth, on the edge of the Yorkshire moors, the daughter of a clergyman and one of six children whose mother died when Charlotte was very young. She and her surviving siblings, Branwell, Emily, and Anne, filled their childhood with elaborate invented kingdoms, writing miniature books in minuscule handwriting, and all three sisters went on to publish novels within roughly a year of one another.\n\nMuch of *Jane Eyre* is built from experience. Charlotte and three of her sisters were sent to a school for clergymen’s daughters where conditions were harsh; the two eldest, Maria and Elizabeth, fell ill and died, and the Lowood chapters carry the anger of someone who was there. She later worked as a teacher and as a governess, positions she found stifling, and she studied and taught for a period in Brussels, an episode that left its mark on the emotional temperature of her fiction.\n\nThe sisters published first as Currer, Ellis, and Acton Bell, choosing deliberately ambiguous names because, as Charlotte later explained, they suspected that work known to be written by women would be judged by a different standard. *Jane Eyre* appeared in 1847 and was an immediate success, though some reviewers found its heroine coarse and its passion improper. Charlotte outlived every one of her siblings, married her father’s curate late, and died at the age of thirty-eight while pregnant, leaving *Jane Eyre* as the book that fixed her among the essential English novelists.',
