          'The animals of the farm sort themselves quickly into those who work and those who supervise, and the sorting never reverses. The pigs and the dogs produce no food; the horses, the hens, the sheep, and the cows produce all of it; and the surplus flows uphill under a series of explanations. Orwell, a socialist writing against a state that called itself socialist, is attacking the arrangement rather than the vocabulary, and the fable would work just as well on a farm that called itself anything else.\n\nBoxer is the argument compressed into one animal. He is the strongest creature on the farm and the only thing he lacks is any capacity for suspicion, so that his loyalty is precisely what makes him exploitable and his slogan about working harder is exactly what the pigs need him to believe. When his body fails he is converted into cash within the week. Orwell puts the knacker’s van into the story in order to make an economic relationship visible in a single image.\n\nThe hardest note in the book is Benjamin, the donkey who sees everything clearly and does nothing. He knows the pigs are lying, he can read as well as they can, and his cynicism protects him without helping anybody else. Orwell leaves no comfortable position available: ignorance is fatal, faith is fatal, and clear-sighted resignation is merely survivable, which leaves the reader to work out what would actually have made a difference.',
