        heading: 'Allegory and the "fairy story"',
        body:
          'Orwell subtitled the book "A Fairy Story," and the label is doing serious work. Fairy tales are short, plainly told, populated by types rather than personalities, and morally unambiguous, and by adopting the form Orwell found a way to write about show trials and purges that required no footnotes. A reader who knows nothing about Trotsky still understands exactly what happens to Snowball, and a reader who does know watches the historical parallels click into place one after another.\n\nThe allegory is unusually tight. Old Major supplies the theory and dies before the revolution; the boar who takes power builds a personal cult and purges his rival; the propagandist rewrites the past; the working animal is used up and sold; the neighboring farmers are hostile until business becomes convenient. Even the timber deal and the sudden betrayal that follows it correspond to events readers in 1945 had lived through and could not mistake.\n\nAllegory that precise usually dies with its occasion, and this one did not. The reason is that Orwell chose to dramatize a process rather than a period, and the sequence by which a promise becomes a privilege and then a lie is available to any regime in any decade. The fairy-story surface, which began as a device for getting past both censors and inattentive readers, turned out to be what made the book portable.',
