      'George Orwell was the pen name of Eric Arthur Blair (1903–1950), born in India to a family he later placed in the lower reaches of the English upper middle class, educated on a scholarship at Eton, and employed for five years as a colonial policeman in Burma. He resigned from that post having concluded that imperialism was indefensible, and spent the following years deliberately living among the poor in Paris and London, which produced his first book and established the pattern of a career built on going to see for himself.\n\nThe decisive experience was Spain. Orwell went in 1936 to fight against Franco, was shot through the throat, and then found himself hunted by the Soviet-backed communists who were suppressing the militia he had joined. He came home convinced that the Soviet Union was not a workers’ state and that many Western intellectuals did not want to hear it, and he wrote afterward that every serious line he produced from then on was written against totalitarianism and for democratic socialism.\n\n*Animal Farm* was written during the Second World War and turned down by several publishers, at least in part because the Soviet Union was then Britain’s ally and an attack on Stalin was unwelcome. It finally appeared in 1945 and made him famous. He followed it with *Nineteen Eighty-Four*, completed while he was seriously ill with tuberculosis, and died a few months after its publication at the age of forty-six, leaving two short books that between them supplied much of the political vocabulary of the century that followed.',
